    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "According to the World Health Organization, more than 300 million people worldwide suffer from type 2 diabetes. Recent studies have demonstrated a previously unsuspected crosstalk between endothelial cells and hepatocytes where Vascular Endothelial Growth Factor (VEGF) maintains the liver vascular network and suppresses hepatic insulin signaling. Conversely, pharmacologic VEGF inhibitors regress the liver sinusoidal vessels, induce relative hypoxia, and increase hepatic insulin signaling through Hypoxia-Inducible Factor (HIF) induction of the insulin receptor adaptor protein IRS2 and suppression of gluconeogenic genes. Here, we explore whether VEGF inhibitors, which potentiate insulin signaling, can be combined with the first-line type 2 diabetes drug metformin, which acts in an insulin-independent manner, to achieve additive or synergistic reductions in blood glucose levels in diabetic db/db mice. Mice treated with combination therapy exhibited improved glucose tolerance in the peripheral blood compared to either single treatment. Moreover, a low dose of the VEGF inhibitor aflibercept was sufficient to induce hepatic vascular regression and to elicit additive improvements in hyperglycemia and glucose tolerance when administered together with oral metformin therapy. However, qRT-PCR analyses of whole liver lysates indicated only mild additive effects at the level of gluconeogenic gene expression; thus, additional mechanisms must contribute to the combinatorial phenotype. These studies suggest that, when given in the context of metformin and at low dosage, VEGF inhibitors may provide a clinically attractive strategy to reduce the pathologically high blood glucose levels seen in type 2 diabetes. This result could be of particular benefit to patients with refractory type 2 diabetes in which metformin is not sufficient for glycemic control.",
